Day 234/250: Separate Squares II

Problem Statement #3454

Problem Statement Explantion:

Given a 2D array squares where squares[i] = [xi, yi, li], representing a square with the bottom-left corner at (x^i,y^i) and side length l^i.
The squares may overlap. 
You need to find a horizontal line y=y such that the total area of the union of the squares below the line equals the total area of the union of the squares above the line. 
Return the value of y as a double.

Solution Approach:

Simply summing individual square areas fails because overlaps must only be counted once. We treat this as a Union of Rectangles problem.

1. The Geometry Logic
Instead of a standard binary search on the y-coordinate (which is computationally expensive when re-calculating union area), we use a Sweep Line along the y-axis.
We collect all y^start and y^end coordinates as "events."
As we move from one y-event to the next, the set of active x-intervals remains constant.
The area of a horizontal "slice" is: Union Length of Active X-intervals×(y^current −y^previous).

2. Segment Tree for X-Intervals
To efficiently find the "Union Length" of overlapping x-intervals:
We use Coordinate Compression on all x-coordinates because they can be very large.
A Segment Tree is built over these compressed x-coordinates.

Each node in the tree stores:
count: How many squares currently cover this interval.
length: The total length within this range covered by at least one square.
When count > 0, the length of the node is the full width of its range. When count == 0, it is the sum of its children's lengths.

3. Finding the Median Line
First Pass: Run the sweep line to calculate the Total Area of the union.

Target Area: Define target = Total Area / 2.
Second Pass: Run the sweep line again, accumulating area slice by slice.
Interpolation: When adding a slice would exceed the target, the median line y must lie within that slice.

Here's the code:

import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public double separateSquares(int[][] squares) {
        List<int[]> events = new ArrayList<>();
        TreeSet<Integer> xCoords = new TreeSet<>();
        for (int[] s : squares) {
            int x1 = s[0], x2 = s[0] + s[2], y1 = s[1], y2 = s[1] + s[2];
            events.add(new int[]{y1, 1, x1, x2});
            events.add(new int[]{y2, -1, x1, x2});
            xCoords.add(x1);
            xCoords.add(x2);
        }
        // Sort events by Y coordinate
        Collections.sort(events, (a, b) -> Integer.compare(a[0], b[0]));

        List<Integer> sortedX = new ArrayList<>(xCoords);
        Map<Integer, Integer> xToIdx = new HashMap<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < sortedX.size(); i++) xToIdx.put(sortedX.get(i), i);

        int m = sortedX.size();
        long[] count = new long[4 * m];
        double[] length = new double[4 * m];

        // Pass 1: Calculate Total Union Area
        double totalArea = 0;
        double prevY = events.get(0)[0];
        for (int[] e : events) {
            totalArea += length[1] * (e[0] - prevY);
            update(1, 0, m - 2, xToIdx.get(e[2]), xToIdx.get(e[3]) - 1, e[1], count, length, sortedX);
            prevY = e[0];
        }

        // Pass 2: Find the Y-line that splits the area in half
        double target = totalArea / 2.0;
        double currentArea = 0;
        Arrays.fill(count, 0);
        Arrays.fill(length, 0);
        prevY = events.get(0)[0];

        for (int[] e : events) {
            double sliceArea = length[1] * (e[0] - prevY);
            if (currentArea + sliceArea >= target) {
                // The target line is within this Y-interval
                return prevY + (target - currentArea) / length[1];
            }
            currentArea += sliceArea;
            update(1, 0, m - 2, xToIdx.get(e[2]), xToIdx.get(e[3]) - 1, e[1], count, length, sortedX);
            prevY = e[0];
        }
        return prevY;
    }

    private void update(int node, int start, int end, int l, int r, int val, long[] count, double[] length, List<Integer> sortedX) {
        if (l > end || r < start) return;
        if (l <= start && end <= r) {
            count[node] += val;
        } else {
            int mid = (start + end) / 2;
            update(node * 2, start, mid, l, r, val, count, length, sortedX);
            update(node * 2 + 1, mid + 1, end, l, r, val, count, length, sortedX);
        }
        
        if (count[node] > 0) {
            length[node] = (double)sortedX.get(end + 1) - sortedX.get(start);
        } else if (start != end) {
            length[node] = length[node * 2] + length[node * 2 + 1];
        } else {
            length[node] = 0;
        }
    }
}

Complexity:

Time Complexity: O(NlogN)
Space Complexity: O(N)



